CONSONANT CLUSTER A consonant cluster is when two or more consonants are positioned together. They can appear at the initial or final position of a word. Consonant cluster is also known as consonant blend. Consonant Cluster vs Consonant Digraph • A consonant cluster is a group or sequence of consonants that appear together in a syllable without a vowel between them. In consonant cluster each letter represents a single sound sp eak, sk y, st ick, sn ail, sw im, • Consonant digraphs are groups of two consonants that make a single sound. Examples of consonant digraphs are: ch in chat sh in ship th in thick wh in what ph in phone ck in sock Examples of Consonant clusters Initial consonant clusters [pl], [pr], [bl], [br] • plead – please – pleasant – plaza – plot – plural – plump – place – plague – plywood – plosive; • preach – priest – pretty – prepare – prefer – print – prison – press – present – pregnant – practice; • bleed – bleak – blink – bliss – bless – blend – black – blank – blast – block – blond – blue – • breed – breeze – breathe – brief – bring – brick – brim – bread – breast – brand – bracket – bra – [tr], [dr] • tree – treat – trim – trip – trick – trend – tread – track – trap – travel – tram – trauma – troop; • dream – drill – drink – drip – draft – drama – draw – drop – droop – drew – drum – drunk – drain; [kl], [kr], [gl], [gr] • clean – clear – click – cling – clever – cleft – clan – class – clasp – clue – clerk – club – claim; • cream – creed – critical – crib – credit – crest – crack – crash – crawl – cross – crew; cruise; • glee – glib – glimpse – glare – glad – glamor – glance – gloomy – glum – glide – globe – global; • green – grief – grease – grip – grim – grin – grab – gram – grand – grasp – grew – groom – group; [fl], [fr] • flee – fleece – flick – flint – flip – flesh – flex – flare – flap – flax – floor – flock – floss – flew; • free – freeze – freak – frequent – frisk – frigid – Fred – friend – fresh – Frank – fraction; [θr], [shr] • three – 